  "title": "CQRS Pattern",
  "description": "Implement CQRS with separate read and write models.",
  "order": 3,
  "difficulty": "advanced",
  "estimatedMinutes": 19,
  "tags": ["cqrs", "read-model", "write-model"],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation) separates the read model from the write model. Each is optimized for its purpose."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Key Concepts"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "key-terms",
      "data": {
        "terms": [
          {
            "term": "CQRS",
            "definition": "Separating command (write) and query (read) models."
          },
          {
            "term": "Command",
            "definition": "An operation that changes state (write)."
          },
          {
            "term": "Query",
            "definition": "An operation that reads state (read)."
          },
          {
            "term": "Read Model",
            "definition": "A denormalized model optimized for queries."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "CQRS Components"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "Command side: handles writes, validates, produces events",
          "Query side: handles reads from optimized read model",
          "Synchronization: read model is eventually consistent with write model"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Benefits"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "Read and write can scale independently",
          "Read model optimized for queries",
          "Write model optimized for business rules",
          "Better performance for read-heavy workloads"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Challenges"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "Eventual consistency between models",
          "More complex architecture",
          "Synchronization overhead",
          "Debugging distributed reads"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "When to Use CQRS"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "Read-heavy workloads",
          "Complex business rules on writes",
          "Need to optimize read and write separately",
          "Combined with event sourcing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Architecture"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "mermaid",
      "data": {
        "id": "msa-cqrs",
        "caption": "CQRS Architecture",
        "definition": "flowchart TD\n    Client[Client] --> Cmd[Command Handler]\n    Client --> Query[Query Handler]\n    Cmd -->|Events| ES[(Event Store)]\n    Cmd -->|Publish| Bus[(Event Bus)]\n    Bus -->|Update| RM[(Read Model)]\n    Query -->|Read| RM"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Best Practices"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "Design for failure",
          "Keep services loosely coupled",
          "Monitor and observe everything",
          "Automate testing and deployment"
        ]
      }
    },
